<p>In the latest phase of the nine-day Israel-Iran war, strikes have widened to critical civilian infrastructure. Overnight attacks hit oil storage depots and refineries around Tehran, sparking major fires and leaving parts of the capital under thick, toxic smoke and oil-tainted rainfall. Iran has also targeted infrastructure in Gulf states, with attacks reported in Bahrain and Kuwait as the conflict’s effects spread across the region. The escalation is now rippling through global energy and shipping. Bahrain’s state oil company declared force majeure after an attack, while broader disruptions include the shutdown of Qatar’s liquefied natural gas facilities and steep declines in Iraqi production, tightening supply as oil prices climbed above $114 a barrel.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/bL3ugE_i_7DnDOngN-AqyD7kngiJjwgsGXyIp1aNCjDWkg0ro7ranHNERF2MBKICsAAofNiJbo6ohY4-w6HgvKGCkf1z0dhLbnjWW_fI' alt='Dark smoke and pollution hang over Tehran after strikes on oil facilities.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Tehran conditions: Reporting described skies over the capital darkening as smoke blotted out the sun, with fires still smouldering more than 12 hours after strikes hit fuel sites.</li><li>Water facilities: Analysts quoted in reporting said attacks on desalination plants in Iran and Bahrain mark a serious escalation, because Gulf countries rely heavily on desalination for drinking water.</li><li>Supply spillover beyond southern Iraq: Several companies in Iraqi Kurdistan halted production as a precaution, cutting about 200,000 bpd that had flowed to Turkey in February, according to reporting.</li><li>Regional fallout: Separate reporting said Saudi Arabia recorded its first deaths as the war’s targets expanded into civilian infrastructure.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Bahrain state media: Bahrain’s state news agency relayed Bapco’s announcement that it is declaring force majeure on shipments after the attack, describing the disruption as extraordinary circumstances that affect contractual obligations. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/bahrains-state-oil-company-declares-force-majeure-on-its-shipments-after-iran-attack/article70721442.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Iranian officials: Provincial authorities said fuel supplies were temporarily interrupted and queues grew at stations as Tehran dealt with the aftermath of strikes on storage and refinery sites, according to reporting. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1979990/oil-rain-falls-on-tehran-after-apocalyptic-strikes-on-refineries'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iranian president: President Masoud Pezeshkian said Iran was “forced to respond” as it launched missiles toward Gulf neighbors, according to reporting. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1979990/oil-rain-falls-on-tehran-after-apocalyptic-strikes-on-refineries'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Arab League: The Arab League condemned what it called “reckless” Iranian attacks as diplomatic efforts faltered, according to the account of regional reactions. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1979990/oil-rain-falls-on-tehran-after-apocalyptic-strikes-on-refineries'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/bahrains-state-oil-company-declares-force-majeure-on-its-shipments-after-iran-attack/article70721442.ece'>Bahrain&#x27;s state oil company declares force majeure on its shipments after Iran attack</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1979990/oil-rain-falls-on-tehran-after-apocalyptic-strikes-on-refineries'>‘Oil rain’ falls on Tehran after apocalyptic strikes on refineries</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/gallery/2026/3/8/aftermath-in-iran-of-us-and-israeli-air-strikes'>Aftermath in Iran of US and Israeli air strikes</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/iran-strike-hits-bahrains-biggest-oil-refinery-bapco-thick-smoke-seen-over-site-watch-13987575.html'>Iran strike hits Bahrain’s 